  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is architecture a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Architecture remains a genuinely viable, growing profession in India, and it is not disappearing.
    Over 1.2 lakh architects are registered with the Council of Architecture as of early 2026, and
    India's construction sector is projected to grow from roughly $0.79 trillion to $1.10 trillion by
    2031.
  </p>

  <p>
    But "good career" does not mean "any B.Arch seat, any college, guaranteed design-studio glamour."
  </p>

  <p>
    It means: real demand exists in specific, growing lanes, real pay exists for graduates who build
    toward those lanes and register with the Council of Architecture, and a genuinely slower, less
    certain first few years exist too, especially for the generic CAD-production and drafting roles most
    B.Arch students default into without a plan.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "architecture is a prestigious, artsy career that pays well because buildings
      matter" story that draws a lot of 12th-graders in. It is also not the doom-scroll version claiming
      architecture is a dying field because junior pay looks weak next to computer science. Both are
      wrong. The honest middle: a genuinely rewarding, growing field for someone who wants design
      authority and is willing to build toward it patiently, and a genuinely rough five to seven years
      for someone who picked it for prestige and expected fast money.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-asking">Why this question hits differently for architecture</h2>

  <p>
    Compared with most engineering branches, this question carries three extra layers that families
    rarely weigh together: an extra year of training, a mandatory apprenticeship most students are not
    paid fairly for, and a legal licensing wall most 12th-graders have never even heard of.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What actually makes architecture different</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>B.Arch is a five-year degree, one year longer than B.Tech, but fresher architect pay runs consistently below fresher engineering pay across most salary trackers.</li>
      <li>Every B.Arch student must complete a minimum 100-day training internship to graduate, and pay for this mandatory period ranges from nothing to a few thousand rupees a month, a well-documented sore point across Indian architecture schools.</li>
      <li>Unlike most engineering branches, architecture has a hard legal licensing wall: Council of Architecture registration under the Architects Act, 1972, without which you cannot legally use the title "Architect" or sign off on drawings.</li>
      <li>At the same time, India's construction spend, BIM adoption, and a severe undersupply of urban and town planners are all creating real new demand inside and around architecture, faster than most families realise.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      Two true things at once: the "architecture is glamorous and well paid because you design buildings"
      version of this decision does not survive contact with real fresher salary data, and the profession
      is simultaneously growing in construction, BIM, planning, and real estate, just not evenly across
      every college, city, or lane.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Both of those headlines are accurate. That combination, extra training year, licensing wall, and real
    growth sitting side by side, is exactly why this question causes more confusion for architecture than
    it does for most engineering branches.
  </p>

  <h2 id="daily-work">What an architect actually does all day</h2>

  <p>
    Before comparing pay or specialisations, it helps to see the actual daily texture of the work, not
    the version that shows up in a college brochure or a design portfolio reel.
  </p>

  <p>
    The boring 80% of architecture is code-compliance checks, drawing revisions, client meetings that
    change the brief again, vendor and contractor coordination, site visits to check that a build matches
    a drawing, and documentation. Concept design, the part everyone imagines from architecture school, is
    real, but it is a small, front-loaded slice of the actual project timeline. Most working weeks in a
    firm involve production drawings, detailing, and problem-solving around a design that was already
    approved months earlier.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      If the appeal of architecture is mainly the idea of sketching bold concepts and seeing a building
      rise exactly as imagined, that happens for a small share of projects and a smaller share of
      architects, usually years into a career. The people who genuinely enjoy the profession usually
      enjoy the whole cycle: revision, compliance, coordination, and the slow craft of getting a design
      built close to intent, not just the opening sketch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="coa-licensing">The part most 12th-graders never hear: COA registration</h2>

  <p>
    This is the single biggest structural difference between architecture and almost every other
    engineering-adjacent branch, and it rarely comes up in career counselling conversations.
  </p>

  <p>
    Getting in is its own decision first. Most COA-recognised B.Arch colleges admit through NATA, a
    drawing, aptitude, and design-reasoning test conducted by the Council of Architecture, with a minimum
    45% aggregate in 12th (PCM) or a 10+3 diploma with maths also required. A smaller set of institutes,
    including the IITs offering B.Arch (Roorkee, Kharagpur) and NITs with architecture programmes, admit
    through JEE Main Paper 2 instead. Well-known COA-recognised schools such as SPA Delhi, SPA Bhopal, SPA
    Vijayawada, CEPT Ahmedabad, and JJ College of Architecture Mumbai each have their own mix of entrance
    routes and cutoffs, so checking the specific exam a target college actually accepts matters more than
    assuming NATA alone is universal.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under the Architects Act, 1972, anyone who wants to practise as an "Architect" in India, use that
    title, or sign off on architectural drawings must register with the Council of Architecture. This is
    not optional professional polish; it is a legal requirement. Practising or advertising as an architect
    without registration exposes a person to real penalties, and it can make professional contracts
    unenforceable.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Registration is only available to graduates of institutions the Council of Architecture recognises.
      A B.Arch from a college that is not COA-recognised can leave a graduate legally unable to register
      and practise as an architect at all, regardless of how good the education felt. Checking a college's
      current COA recognition status before admission matters more than its brand name, its ranking, or
      its campus photos.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The training path itself is also stricter than most branches: every B.Arch student must complete a
    minimum 100-day training internship as part of the COA-mandated curriculum, on top of coursework.
    Documented reports from Indian architecture graduates describe stipends ranging from nothing at all to
    roughly Rs 4,000-15,000 a month for genuinely long working days, a gap that architecture bodies and
    graduates themselves have called out publicly as an unresolved fairness problem in the profession.
    Families weighing this branch should budget for that reality directly instead of discovering it
    mid-degree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-reality">Real pay, track by track</h2>

  <p>
    "Architect salary in India" is close to a meaningless single number, because the gap between a
    generic CAD-production offer and a BIM-specialised, government, or independent-practice track is
    enormous, inside the exact same five-year degree.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Track</th>
          <th>Typical pay</th>
          <th>Why the gap exists</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((item) => (
